e  simple greeting greeting such as  the speaker a says good morning and the  speaker  b would answer morning or it can be a  question answered pair  the speaker a would say do you like  movies  and the speaker b would respond yes i do  chaining these pairs together can create  a naturally flowing conversation  we want our sub dialogues to be made of  one or several  adjacency pairs this way it is simpler  for us to predict  user responses due to adjacency pairs  often having expected specific types of  answers  first we wanted to test how prevalent  adjacency pairs are in conversation  to see how viable for building a  dialogue from them be  in the first place we analyzed all  conversations in the multiverse data set  for each entry in the conversation we  have noted the dialog  act of the entry and the dialog act of  the following entry as well  as noted who is currently speaking we  have then created a list of dialogue act  pairs  that fulfill all four previously  mentioned conditions  and we use them as adjacency power  candidates  the results show that as expected  the dialogues have the adjacency pair  structure  for example almost one four of  statements  made by one speaker are immediately  followed by some sort of minor  acknowledgement  by the second speaker or and the first  speaker  asks a w question this question is  in 59 of cases followed by a statement  usually the answer by the second speaker  these patterns correspond to adjacency  pairs as described previously  we aim to recreate a similar structure  of conversation within our system  where conversation consists of adjacency  pairs  that are then chained together to  provide an engaging and natural  conversation  we have defined a set of adjacency  groups  where each group contains a set of  similar adjacency pairs  with the same opening utterance but  different closing curtains  you can see these examples on the screen  there are 17 groups in total  and they are divided into two categories  user-